#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Runner for the scheduled L1 programmes (one backlog task per run).

Usage: ark-l1 [--program pii|dlp] SUBCOMMAND   (default: $ARK_L1_PROGRAM or pii)

Programmes share the clone, the cargo targets and the gate venv under ~/ark-l1; each keeps its
own state, stack, worktrees, dev data and holdout (pii: ~/ark-l1, dlp: ~/ark-dlp).

Isolation: the agent works in its own clone, never in a worktree of the shared repository.
Everything that executes agent-controlled code or reads agent-controlled git metadata (gate steps,
git status/bundle in the agent clone) runs in a systemd sandbox without credentials. Agent commits
reach the trusted repository only as a bundle; publish builds the "done" commit with plumbing in
the trusted repository and pushes from there.

Subcommands:
  next            restack the programme's open PRs, pick one task, create the agent clone; a rebase
                  that does not apply cleanly becomes the run's task (RESTACK:<branch>), resolved
                  by the agent, gated like any task and force-pushed with a lease by `publish`
  env             print the current run as shell assignments
  ahead           print the number of commits in the agent clone (sandboxed)
  stale           review/restack runs: exit 0 and drop the run when its PR branch moved or was
                  deleted on GitHub since the run started (a person pushed or merged); else 1
  gate            import the agent commits and run the independent checks (sandboxed)
  ci              push the would-be PR head to the branch and wait for GitHub CI on it
                  (0 = green, 1 = red with the failed logs in gate-report.md, 3 = no verdict)
  publish         open a ready PR on the stack, only for a head with green GitHub CI (idempotent)
  latency         verify runs (VERIFY:<branch>): quiet latency A/B of the PR head against main, under
                  the run lock, before the agent round
  verdict         verify runs: read the agent's verdict; pass lets auto-merge merge that exact head,
                  fail posts the findings as review threads (two failed verifies block the PR)
  block ID REASON skip a task until `unblock`; drops the current worktree
  abort           drop the current worktree without blocking the task
  unblock ID      make a blocked task selectable again
  status          print stack, blocked tasks and the next candidate
  idle            exit 0 when the programme's last `next` found nothing to do (NOTHING_TO_DO,
                  NO_BACKLOG); run.sh starts a second programme only then (ARK_L1_WHEN_IDLE)
  landable        exit 0 when `next` has work on this lane's open PRs (merge, verify, restack,
                  review); run.sh then starts the second programme anyway, with ARK_L1_LAND_ONLY=1
  own BRANCH...   print the given task branches that belong to this host's lane

Verify: auto-merge (and `land`) merges a PR that changes ark-core/ only after a verify run passed
on its exact head: a fresh agent checks it against the backlog task's accept criteria and the
CLAUDE.md code rules and writes a verdict; it never commits.

Exit codes of `next`: 0 = task ready, 10 = nothing to do (reason on stdout),
11 = the previous run passed the gate but was not published yet (run `publish`).

Lanes: two hosts can share a programme's backlog (ARK_L1_LANE, see PROGRAMS[...]["lanes"]). A lane
selects only its own tasks and reviews, restacks and merges only their PRs; branch numbers are
dealt round-robin over the lanes, so two hosts never create the same branch. Without ARK_L1_LANE
a host works on every task.
"""

